answer/value "the sum property" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335474/field-3/3fbc166f0ca82f98a387364d8e9c36034073c6b33cb86f09819bccc9f55bb35c" :answer/id #uuid "d3728d1a-cae2-57ab-9be5-91ce3bdecd4f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335474/field-3/cc545c04e2dcad28fd649be5b590cd32108605dca0f2f8781f99422c4ab67505" :answer/id #uuid "fa909451-acb8-58ad-b710-fd08aacd9789" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335474/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "6ab1d23f-0c61-526e-91ed-01d0ec7266e1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-335474/field-3/3fbc166f0ca82f98a387364d8e9c36034073c6b33cb86f09819bccc9f55bb35c" :answer-field/choices ["q-335474/field-3/8fd8091ad80f7d2511ea6ab5fad6d1dd366029d679ddf5d03fdce7b5b86d2f68" "q-335474/field-3/438b4c4d03a21de630e66c77197769846fa4a0ad2f925cfdb3df140268e70810" "q-335474/field-3/747bf59f129b39ed16d0609e880083b7ec7fa22b98988114d7cffd9bba5b175a" "q-335474/field-3/3fbc166f0ca82f98a387364d8e9c36034073c6b33cb86f09819bccc9f55bb35c" "q-335474/field-3/cc545c04e2dcad28fd649be5b590cd32108605dca0f2f8781f99422c4ab67505"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335474/field-4/4aba6ae530a1a9ae6a0e811a31dd556956a43739e9518d9a3cb43c8ffe3000ae" :answer/id #uuid "e489cee6-ef3e-5c3b-9f23-e15b3f9fb26f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{L2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335474/field-4/e04811e52d1b108844992320b02362618b439dac20ffc031cd3e9eda4c7aaa2b" :answer/id #uuid "84d04d3f-0aa5-5192-b892-ae9c470ec6f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{L3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335474/field-4/124b0c2eb11420d91f7684989e46375a3c1c904a04910b2d823439550b297bf2" :answer/id #uuid "aabce835-08a6-56a8-b0f2-22d9384b0c2d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{L4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335474/field-4" :answer-field/id #uuid "f7a2c3a4-8f21-5f68-b437-c71247310fe4" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-335474/field-4/124b0c2eb11420d91f7684989e46375a3c1c904a04910b2d823439550b297bf2" :answer-field/choices ["q-335474/field-4/4aba6ae530a1a9ae6a0e811a31dd556956a43739e9518d9a3cb43c8ffe3000ae" "q-335474/field-4/e04811e52d1b108844992320b02362618b439dac20ffc031cd3e9eda4c7aaa2b" "q-335474/field-4/124b0c2eb11420d91f7684989e46375a3c1c904a04910b2d823439550b297bf2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335474/field-5/8fd8091ad80f7d2511ea6ab5fad6d1dd366029d679ddf5d03fdce7b5b86d2f68" :answer/id #uuid "654fb9e5-f871-5ba7-b444-e894d7aed45b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335474/field-5/438b4c4d03a21de630e66c77197769846fa4a0ad2f925cfdb3df140268e70810" :answer/id #uuid "06004eb1-d6ed-5f92-804c-1bc954b8fd62" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335474/field-5/747bf59f129b39ed16d0609e880083b7ec7fa22b98988114d7cffd9bba5b175a" :answer/id #uuid "9726151e-714b-5a56-8624-2cc1f3c0a4a7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the sum property" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335474/field-5/cc545c04e2dcad28fd649be5b590cd32108605dca0f2f8781f99422c4ab67505" :answer/id #uuid "ea8b9622-d29a-5e20-853b-104361febf53" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335474/field-5/3fbc166f0ca82f98a387364d8e9c36034073c6b33cb86f09819bccc9f55bb35c" :answer/id #uuid "232b2854-bc6f-5c19-b90a-f11da1903761" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335474/field-5" :answer-field/id #uuid "38e7a7e1-04c5-5a86-9ba8-e8743de99f2c" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-335474/field-5/438b4c4d03a21de630e66c77197769846fa4a0ad2f925cfdb3df140268e70810" :answer-field/choices ["q-335474/field-5/8fd8091ad80f7d2511ea6ab5fad6d1dd366029d679ddf5d03fdce7b5b86d2f68" "q-335474/field-5/438b4c4d03a21de630e66c77197769846fa4a0ad2f925cfdb3df140268e70810" "q-335474/field-5/747bf59f129b39ed16d0609e880083b7ec7fa22b98988114d7cffd9bba5b175a" "q-335474/field-5/cc545c04e2dcad28fd649be5b590cd32108605dca0f2f8781f99422c4ab67505" "q-335474/field-5/3fbc166f0ca82f98a387364d8e9c36034073c6b33cb86f09819bccc9f55bb35c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335474" :question/id #uuid "0f486a0b-746a-5894-968d-0df3eeeabb50" :question/math-academy-id "q-335474" :question/problem "Consider the following statement. \n\nIf $f(x)=4{x}^{2}-x+5,$ then $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nA proof of the statement is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,f=O(4{x}^{2}-x+5)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{4{x}^{2}-x}{5})=0$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,4{x}^{2}-x=O(5)$  as $x\\to 0$ \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,f=O(5)$  as $x\\to 0$   \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,f=O(1)$  as $x\\to 0$ \n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ follows from  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from lines  \n{{field-2}}  \nand  \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line  \n{{field-4}}  \nand  \n{{field-5}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x),g(x),h(x)$ and $k(x)$ are functions and $c$ is a nonzero constant. Then, we have the following properties of Big-O notation.\n\n- The sum and product properties. If\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(k)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf+g=O(h+k)\\,\\text{and}\\,fg=O(hk)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The constant multiple property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(cg)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The reflexive property.\n\n$$\nf=O(f)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The transitive property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The absorption property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g+h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let us justify each selected step.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L1},$ which states that\n\n$$\nf=O({{4{x}^{2}-x+5}_{⏟}}_{f}).\n$$\n\nThis is true by $\\text{the reflexive property}.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L4}.$ From line $\\text{L1}$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(4{x}^{2}-x+5),\n$$\n\nand from line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}-x=O(5)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThus, by lines $\\text{L1 and L3}$ and $\\text{the absorption property},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf=O(5)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L4}$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(5)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nSince $5$ is just a constant multiple of $1,$ it follows by $\\text{the constant multiple property}$ that\n\n$$\nf=O(1)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-335474/field-1" "q-335474/field-2" "q-335474/field-3" "q-335474/field-4" "q-335474/field-5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffe4292f-7653-5486-975e-c206acae734e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-335474"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21710"] :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x) = -3x^4 + x^2 + 4x,$ show that $f = \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$" :question/worked-solution "We say $f(x)$ is of order $g(x)$ as $x\\to a,$ and we write \n\n$$\nf(x) = \\mathcal O(g(x)) \\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a\n$$\n\nif there exist ${\\textrm{positive}}$ constants $K$ and $\\delta$ such that\n\n$$\n{|f(x)| \\leq K|g(x)|}\n\\quad\\textrm{for all}\\quad 0 < |x-a| < \\delta.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, in our case, we need to show that\n\n$$\n{|f(x)| \\leq K|x|}\n \\quad \\textrm{for} \\quad {0 < |x| < \\delta}.\n$$\n\nBy the triangle inequality, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|f(x)| & =|-3{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}+4x| \\\\  & \\le |3{x}^{4}|+|{x}^{2}|+|4x| \\\\  & =3|x{|}^{4}+|x{|}^{2}+4|x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $|x| < 1,$ then we have ${|x|^2 \\lt |x|}.$ Also, since $|x|<1,$ we have $|x|^4 < |x|.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n3|x|^4 \\le 3|x|\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n|x|^2 \\le |x|.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|f(x)| & \\le 3|x{|}^{4}+|x{|}^{2}+4|x| \\\\  & \\le 3|x|+|x|+4|x| \\\\  & =8|x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $|f(x)| \\leq 8|x|$ whenever $|x| < 1.$ Thus, taking ${K=8}$ and ${\\delta = 1}$ satisfies the definition, and we conclude that $f = \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-7762"] :question/problem "Suppose $f(x) = e^x + x\\ln x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f= \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to\\infty$\n\n- $f= \\mathcal O(\\ln x)$ as $x\\to\\infty$\n\n- $f= \\mathcal O(xe^{x})$ as $x\\to\\infty$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\mathcal O(g(x)) \\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is either finite or $+\\infty.$ To do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to a} \\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = e^x + x\\ln x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Let $g(x) = x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince direct evaluation leads to the indeterminate form $\\dfrac{\\infty}{\\infty},$ we can evaluate the limit using L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}}{(x)^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}{1} \\\\  & =1+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =1+\\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f \\neq \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to\\infty.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let $g(x) =\\ln x.$  Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}{1/x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }x({e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f \\neq \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to\\infty.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let $g(x) = xe^{x}.$ Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x}+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =0+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}}{({e}^{x})^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1/x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f = \\mathcal O(xe^x)$ as $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21715"] :question/problem "Consider the following statement. \n\nIf $f(x) = e^{x}+ \\sec x,$ then $f = \\mathcal O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nA sketch of a proof of this statement is given below\n\n$\\textrm{L1}{:}\\; e^{x} = \\mathcal O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$ \n\n$\\textrm{L2}{:}\\; \\sec x = \\mathcal O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$ \n\n$\\textrm{L3}{:}\\; f = \\mathcal O(2)$ as $x\\to 0$   \n\n$\\textrm{L4}{:}\\; f = \\mathcal O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$   \n\nFill in the blanks in the reasoning below that justify each step.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L3}$ follows from lines  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/assets/e52e49265a91cd725c6c5ffb89764144b14f9c7ecd39d8f72c4341337c985492.png) and  the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/assets/04029e3a400b01eb4e063bc8609fc8724b1f9c0b0c0291194e330aead3dee346.png) property.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L4}$ follows from line ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/assets/69fd9b3718fc120ca9106dcb8c7d74154071ba2454634d3b3a2cc59aa64a522c.png) and the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/assets/819c819776622cbb5c29604cb7c5a6ef3bff8554912b649391b8b4f482aa36b0.png)." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x), g(x), h(x)$ and $k(x)$ are functions and $c$ is a nonzero constant. Then, we have the following properties of Big-O notation.\n\n- The sum and product properties. If\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad g = \\mathcal O(k)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf+g = \\mathcal O(h+k)\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad fg = \\mathcal O(hk)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The constant multiple property. If\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(cg)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(g)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The reflexive property.\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(f)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The transitive property. If\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(g) \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad g = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The absorption property. If\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(g+h)\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad g = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let us justify each selected step.\n\n- We first consider line  $\\textrm{L3}.$ From line $\\textrm{L1}$ we have\n\n$$\ne^{x} = \\mathcal O(1)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0,\n$$\n\nand from line $\\textrm{L2}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sec x = \\mathcal O(1)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThus, by lines $\\bbox[4pt,border:solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{L1 and L3}}$ and $\\bbox[4pt,border:solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{the sum property}},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(1+1) = \\mathcal O(2)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0.\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\textrm{L4}.$ From line $\\bbox[4pt,border:solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{L4}}$ we have\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(2)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nSince $2$ is just a constant multiple of $1,$ it follows by $\\bbox[4pt,border:solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{the constant multiple property}}$ that\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(1)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
